    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Choose one next planning step using the local decision below. Return exactly one listed statement and stop. No other planning choices have been supplied.\n{\"context\": \"The team has a wait log, reader feedback, and a short closing discussion.\", \"decision\": \"How should staff choose one improvement for the next afternoon?\", \"goal\": \"Keep books moving and make the afternoon service easier to use without excluding readers who arrive without an appointment.\", \"problem\": \"A small library becomes crowded during afternoon pickups and returns. It has a request sheet, adjustable pickup windows, a few trained helpers, labeled shelves, and enough room to separate several activities. Staff want a practical plan that preserves walk-in access. Each decision below concerns a different part of the service; none requires exclusive use of all helpers or space.\"}\nCANDIDATE STATEMENTS:\nStaff identify the most repeated concern in reader feedback and check the wait log before choosing one improvement. The improvement begins with reader experience and checks its operational pattern.\nStaff identify the longest waits in the log and use reader feedback to choose one improvement during closing. The improvement begins with a measured bottleneck and checks its effect on readers.\nStaff propose one improvement during closing and compare both the wait log and reader feedback before agreeing to try it. The proposed change must fit both kinds of evidence."
    }
